LIST M1-c064 L04 None | How is one currency unit (CU) related to cents in this unit? | 1 CU is 100 cents, and amounts in cents are whole numbers.
LIST M1-c065 L04 None | What is the periodic factor F_m(r) for a nominal annual rate r compounded m times a year? | F_m(r) = (1 + r/m)^m, the one-year growth factor (Definition 1.1).
LIST M1-c066 L04 None | What is the effective annual rate EAR_m(r), as defined in Definition 1.1? | EAR_m(r) = F_m(r) − 1, a decimal growth rate over one year.
LIST M1-c067 L04 None | What is the continuously compounded factor over t years at rate r? | e^(rt) (Definition 1.2).
LIST M1-c068 L04 None | What is the growth factor over t years with m periods a year at nominal rate r? | (1 + r/m)^(mt), because mt is the number of periods (Remark 1.3).
LIST M1-c069 L04 T | For r > 0, how does F_m(r) change as the number of periods m grows? | It is strictly increasing over whole m ≥ 1, and so is EAR_m(r) (Proposition 2.1).
LIST M1-c070 L04 T | For r > 0, is F_m(r) < e^r for every whole m ≥ 1? | Yes, under Assumption E (1 + x < e^x for x > 0, an assumption of Lesson 4). Use it at x = r/m, then raise to the power m (Proposition 2.2).
LIST M1-c071 L04 T | What is e^(r(s + t)) in terms of e^(rs) and e^(rt)? | e^(rs) e^(rt), for every real r and every s, t ≥ 0 (Proposition 3.1).
LIST M1-c072 L04 T | For r > 0, what nominal rate compounded m times a year has one-year factor e^r? | r_m = m(e^(r/m) − 1) when 1 + r_m/m > 0 (Problem R3).
LIST M1-c073 L04 C | What is the effective annual rate of a 5% nominal rate compounded monthly? | 0.0511618979, which is (1 + 0.05/12)^12 − 1. It is above the nominal 0.05 (Lab 1, Part A).
LIST M1-c074 L04 C | On 1,000,000 cents for ten years at 5%, which step adds more cents: annual to monthly, or daily to continuous? | Annual to monthly adds 18,114 cents. Daily to continuous adds 56 cents (Lab 1, Part C).
LIST M1-c075 L04 T | Can more frequent compounding push F_m(r) above e^r for r > 0? | No. Under Assumption E, every F_m(r) is below e^r (Proposition 2.2), although each extra credit adds value (Proposition 2.1).
LIST M1-c076 L04 C | What is the value of 1,000,000 cents after ten years at a continuous 5%, in whole cents? | 1,648,721 cents, from 1,000,000 × e^0.5 = 1,648,721.27 cents (Example 3.2).
LIST M1-c077 L05 None | What is the annual effective discount factor for time t at rate r? | (1 + r)^(−t), the value today of one CU due at t (Definition 1.1).
LIST M1-c078 L05 None | What is the discount factor for time t with m equal periods a year at nominal rate r? | (1 + r/m)^(−mt) (Definition 1.1).
LIST M1-c079 L05 None | What is the continuous discount factor for time t at rate r? | e^(−rt) (Definition 1.1).
LIST M1-c080 L05 None | What is the present value of an amount C due at time t? | C · DF(t), in the units of C (Definition 1.2).
LIST M1-c081 L05 None | What is the present value of amounts C_k due at times t_k? | The sum of C_k · DF(t_k) over k, with DF under the stated convention (Definition 1.2).
LIST M1-c082 L05 None | Does this lesson define how a pair of dates becomes a year fraction t? | No. Each t is an input number of years, and a different day count gives a different factor (Remark 1.3).
LIST M1-c083 L05 T | For r > 0 and t > 0, where does (1 + r)^(−t) lie? | Strictly between 0 and 1, under Assumption E (Proposition 1.4).
LIST M1-c084 L05 T | What is the equivalent continuous rate δ of an annual effective rate r, and what identity holds? | δ = ln(1 + r), and (1 + r)^(−t) = e^(−δt) for every t ≥ 0 (Proposition 2.1).
LIST M1-c085 L05 T | For r > 0 and t > 0, is e^(−rt) smaller than (1 + r)^(−t)? | Yes, under Assumption E. A continuous rate equal to r discounts more than an annual effective rate equal to r (Proposition 2.2).
LIST M1-c086 L05 T | For r > 0 and t > 0, is the semiannual factor (1 + r/2)^(−2t) below the annual effective factor (1 + r)^(−t)? | Yes, strictly, under Assumption E (Proposition 2.4).
LIST M1-c087 L05 T | Can a discount factor exceed one when the rate is positive? | No. Under Assumption E, for r > 0 and t > 0, 0 < (1 + r)^(−t) < 1 (Proposition 1.4).
LIST M1-c088 L05 T | Is a semiannual nominal 5% the same discount factor as an annual effective 5% at t = 5? | No. The semiannual factor is strictly smaller (Proposition 2.4), so the two conventions give different discount factors at t = 5.
LIST M1-c089 L05 T | Is the continuous factor at 5% below the annual effective factor at 5% for every t > 0? | Yes. Under Assumption E, e^(−0.05t) < 1.05^(−t) for every t > 0 (Proposition 2.2).
LIST M1-c090 L05 C | At t = 1, what do Lab 1's annual and continuous factors at 5% print? | 0.9523809524 for the annual factor and 0.9512294245 for the continuous factor.
LIST M1-c091 L05 C | What is the present value at t = 5 of 1,000,000 cents under annual effective compounding at 5%, in whole cents? | 783,526 cents, from 1,000,000 × 1.05^(−5) = 783,526.17 cents (Example 3.1).
LIST M1-c092 L05 C | What is the equivalent continuous rate of an annual effective rate of 5%, to ten decimals, according to Lab 1? | 0.0487901642, which is ln(1.05).
LIST M1-c144 L04 None | What is Assumption E in Lesson 4, and is it derived from L03? | For every real x > 0, 1 + x < e^x. It is taken as an assumption of the page and is not derived from L03's A1 to A3.
LIST M1-c145 L05 None | How does Lesson 5 define b^u for a positive base b and a real exponent u? | b^u := e^(u ln b). This is a convention of the page; L03 does not define a real power.
LIST M1-c152 L05 C | At t = 5, what is the gap in cents between the annual and semiannual present values of 1,000,000 cents? | 2,328 cents, from 783,526 cents minus 781,198 cents.
LIST M1-c160 L05 None | In Lesson 5, is the exponential bound 1 + x < e^x for x > 0 derived in that lesson? | No. The page takes it as Assumption E and does not derive it from L03's A1 to A3. Propositions 1.4, 2.2 and 2.4 assume it.
LIST M1-c163 L04 T | For r > 0 and whole m ≥ 1, with 1 + r_m/m > 0, is the nominal rate r_m whose one-year factor is e^r above r? | Yes, r_m > r under Assumption E (Problem R3). The condition 1 + r_m/m > 0 is needed, since for even m the root −e^(r/m) gives a negative r_m.
LIST M1-c176 L04 None | What is the effective annual rate of continuous compounding at rate r? | e^r − 1 (Definition 1.2).
LIST M1-c177 L05 T | For r > 0, how does (1 + r)^(−t) move as t increases? | It strictly decreases for t ≥ 0, under Assumption E (Proposition 1.4).
exit 0
